        'synopsis' => 'A boy with a pirate map sails before the mast and must outwit the cleverest rogue ever to sail under the black flag.',
        'text' => "Squire Trelawney, Dr Livesey, and the rest of these gentlemen having asked me to write down the whole particulars of our voyage to Treasure Island, I take up my pen in the year of grace seventeen hundred and sixty, and go back to the time when my father kept the Admiral Benbow inn and the brown old seaman first came under our roof.\n\nI remember him as if it were yesterday. He arrived one day dragging behind him a heavy sea chest up the hill road, a tall strong heavy man with a scar across one cheek and a tarry pigtail falling over his shoulders. He looked at the little cove where our boats lay, looked up at the inn, and sang as he came, that old song that has never since left my memory. Fifteen men on the dead man's chest, yo-ho-ho, and a bottle of rum. He demanded a glass of rum from my father and drank it standing, his eyes fixed on the sea, and then he asked in a kind of growl whether many decent people came this way.\n\nWeeks passed and he stayed, watching the horizon from his seat by the shore as if he waited for someone he feared more than anything on earth. Every night he drank himself stupid with rum, and every morning he stood a while on the beach scanning the offing through his brass telescope. My poor father died quietly in the midst of all this, and I ran the inn alone with my mother, hardly daring to cross our terrible guest.\n\nThen one bitter January evening the man he feared arrived. I was standing at the door of the inn when I saw a figure come slowly up the road in the dark. It was a blind man, feeling his way with a stick, and when he spoke his voice was husky and cold. My friend Bill, or I am much mistaken, he said. Take me in straight to the captain. The old seaman turned white as wax at the sound, for he knew the voice too. The blind stranger took his hand and bent it backward until something fell from it onto the floor, and I understood dimly that some dreadful payment had been made. When I raised the fallen paper I saw it was only a bit of card, but on one side were printed words I did not know, and on the other was written plainly. You have till ten tonight. It was what they called the black spot, and the terror of it killed the captain before midnight, for he fell down in a fit just as the church clock began striking eight.\n\nWe had no time to grieve. Within the hour came the tramp of feet on the lane and a knocking that shook the house, and my mother and I fled out the back with what we could carry, hiding beneath the bridge while seven or eight men broke into the inn. Through a chink I watched them tear open the dead captain's chest and find it empty of the thing they wanted, and then their fury when horsemen came thundering down the lane with pistols. They scattered into the dark like rats, leaving their blind leader groaning alone on the road, run down in his flight.\n\nWhat the pirates wanted we discovered soon enough, for in the captain's coat, which I had carried off in our escape, was a packet stitched tight with oilcloth. Inside lay an old book, a ship's journal, and a fair round map of an island marked with hills and bays and three crosses in red ink, and at the foot of the map written in a bold hand these words. Bulk of treasure here. It was Captain Flint's map, and Flint had buried seven hundred thousand pounds on that island, and the men who broke into our inn had been Flint's own crew hunting it ever since.\n\nDr Livesey and Squire Trelawney read the map over my shoulder, and I saw the doctor's eyes shine. Tomorrow I start for Bristol, cried the squire, and in three weeks we shall have the best ship in England and the pick of the best crews, and Jim you shall come as cabin boy. I shall be admiral, we'll have Livesey for ship's doctor, and nobody must hear a word of where we are going, not a soul.\n\nThe squire kept his word about the ship and broke it about the secret, for he talked of treasure and of Flint in every tavern in Bristol until half the waterfront knew our errand. He hired a fine schooner called the Hispaniola, and he engaged a crew gathered for him by a sea cook he praised mightily, a man recommended as honest and able, an old sailor with one leg, named Long John Silver.\n\nI shall not forget my first sight of that man. He sat outside a Bristol tavern on a barrel of apples, and his crutch tapped the ground as he hopped about among the sailors like a bird. He was tall and strong with a face as big as a ham, plain and pale but intelligent and smiling, and his left leg had been lost above the knee, so that he wore a crutch under his left arm. Silver received us with the respect of a gentleman, and before we sailed I heard him sing to amuse the crew the very song the old buccaneer used to bellow at our inn, and I felt the strangest chill pass over me, though I could not tell why.\n\nWe weighed anchor and made a good voyage toward the Caribbean. Mr Arrow the mate was useless with drink and vanished overboard early, and the captain, a grave man named Smollett, grew daily more troubled in spirit. He told the squire and the doctor plainly that he liked neither the crew nor his own orders. We are all supposed to be sworn friends aboard this ship, he said, but I find things going wrong. These sailors know this voyage before they sign, which no common crew should. There is a man among them with one leg whom I mistrust, and if there is a trap laid for us somewhere, it will spring suddenly.\n\nI learned how sudden two nights later. I had crept into the great apple barrel on deck to hide from the dark and had fallen asleep inside it, and I woke to the voice of Long John Silver speaking softly close beside me. It was Flint's own voice talking mutiny. The plan was simple and cold. Let the squire and the captain find the island and dig the treasure ashore, said Silver, and when the gold was safe aboard, we would shoot the gentlemen like dogs, all except the boy perhaps. As for me, I was to be spared because they liked me, or sold, the speaker did not say which. Then the parrot on his shoulder screamed pieces of eight, pieces of eight, and the crew laughed low around the barrel where I lay frozen with fear.\n\nI slipped from my hiding place when the talk moved aft, and I brought the whole plot to the captain and the doctor before the anchor was down. We held council in the cabin with the door shut. Captain Smollett laid a plan of cool courage that I have admired ever since. The crew would be given shore leave and the guns would be loaded, and the honest men would seize the ship if they could, or fortify a position ashore if they could not.\n\nThe island rose ahead next morning, gray hills and dark pines and three tall peaks marked on our map as the foremast, the mainmast, and the mizzen. Smollett gave the men liberty in boatloads, and while they were away I tumbled into a boat myself on an errand for the cook and leaped overboard the moment we touched the beach. I ran into the trees without looking back, and there among the thickets I came face to face with a horror of shaggy hair and rags that fell on its knees before me. It was a man, white as a cake of soap where the sun had not reached him, and he cried out that he was poor Ben Gunn, the best friend Jim Hawkins ever had. He had been marooned on the island three years alone, he said, living on goats and berries and shellfish, and his one dream was cheese, plain English cheese. He told me wild things in his half crazy way, that he had been with Flint when Flint buried the gold, that another sailor had come seeking it with a party and gone back empty, and that if the squire would promise him a passage home he could show wonders. Above all he repeated again and again that if the pirates landed to dig, they would find nothing but trouble waiting, for he had been beforehand with them.\n\nThe trouble began that very afternoon. Our little band of honest men had carried powder and provisions across to an old stockade on the hill above the best harbor, a log house loopholed for muskets and ringed with a fence six feet high, and there behind it we raised the Union Jack and waited. Toward evening Long John Silver came up under a flag of truce with two followers, smiling and polite as ever, and made us an offer in smooth words. Hand over the map, he said, join my crew, and every man of you shall walk home safe in the Hispaniola. The doctor answered him so coldly and so plainly that the smile died on Silver's face, and he went down the hill swearing vengeance, and within the hour their shots were cracking through the pines.\n\nSeven of us stood off the whole pirate crew that night and the day following. Tom Redruth the gamekeeper was shot dead at the gate in the first skirmish, and I saw my first man die for the sake of gold. The captain himself was wounded, and Joyce and Hunter kept watch through the dark, while Silver's men jeered us from the trees between volleys. We were few enough that every man had his post named aloud, the squire with the best reach of the six muskets at the north corner, the doctor moving from one loophole to another like smoke, and I carrying powder and running messages bent double below the loopholes while the logs splintered overhead. In all my life I never heard such a night wind full of whistling balls and wild halloos, nor thought to come out of it alive, yet when morning came our little flag still flew above the fence, torn but flying.\n\nThen came my own maddest adventure. I found the doctor's little coracle hidden among the rushes where Ben Gunn had told me of it, and at midnight I paddled her out past the sleeping sentries to where the great ship rode at anchor. Her watch was drunk or asleep, and I drifted under her high black stern and heard the two watchmen quarreling far overhead. With my knife I cut the hawser through, and the current swung the mighty vessel slowly round and bore her away from her moorings toward the northern point of the island. When day broke the pirates woke on deck to find their ship adrift in fog, and I, half dead with terror, was clinging near enough to see Israel Hands the gunner left aboard scramble down to fight me. What followed was a battle of wits and steel at the masthead. Hands flung his knife and pinned my shoulder to the mast, and as he lunged I fired both pistols into him, and he fell into the bright green water without a cry.\n\nI got ashore stiff and bleeding and crept back to what I thought was safety, only to walk straight into the log house and into the arms of the enemy. The stockade had fallen in my absence. There sat Long John Silver by the fire with his crutch beside him, and around him the fiercest faces of the mutineer crew, and my friends nowhere. I expected death on the spot, but Silver did a thing that has puzzled me all my life. He whispered to me quickly, Jim, you save me from these men if they turn, and I will stand by you, for I am on your side now. And indeed when morning came and the crew handed him the black spot torn from a Bible, accusing him of letting the prize slip away, he faced them down with the map in his fist and bargained like a lawyer, showing them how the treasure hunt must go forward and how I was worth more alive than dead.\n\nThe doctor came under truce that noon to tend our wounded, and I learned that he and the squire and Ben Gunn were snug in Gunn's secret cave among the pines with plenty of powder and salt meat. Between them they had contrived to give up the old stockade on purpose, and the trap was set.\n\nNext day the whole pirate crew marched inland with picks and shovels, dragging Long John along on his crutch, and I went with them bound hand and foot in spirit though not in rope, for I was Silver's hostage and shield. They climbed toward the high ground called Skeleton Island and struck into a clearing full of gaunt leafless trees, and there they dug like fiends, throwing up the mold in clouds, following the bearings of the red crosses on the chart. Deeper they dug and deeper until the pit gaped taller than a man, and then suddenly from among the pines rang out a thin high voice singing. Fifteen men on the dead man's chest, yo-ho-ho, and a bottle of rum. It was the ghost voice of Captain Flint, or so the superstitious wretches believed, and I knew it for Ben Gunn playing his old trick, though never did a singer do such work with a single verse. The spades dropped from twenty pairs of hands. Some knelt and prayed, some cursed, and one poor wretch fainted dead away, and in the midst of the panic Silver gripped my arm hard and dragged me back from the edge of the pit, for he alone had guessed the truth. There was no treasure in the pit and never had been, for Ben Gunn had dug it up long ago and carried it away on his own back, handful by handful through the years to his cave by the sea.\n\nThe pirates turned on Silver then with murder in their eyes, and George Merry leveled a musket at his head, but before a finger could press trigger there cracked out from the stockade fence a volley of musketry, and the doctor, the squire, Gray the loyal sailor, and Ben Gunn poured their fire into the clearing. George Merry dropped dead, and the rest fled screaming through the trees leaving their picks and shovels scattered in the pit, and that was the end of the fighting on Treasure Island.\n\nWe took three days carrying the gold from Gunn's cave down to the beach, seven hundred thousand pounds of Spanish coin and ingots, loading it in the boats by lamplight, and Long John Silver worked harder than any man of us all the while smiling and serving, always watching his chance. At the last, in a port of Spanish America where we put in for stores, he slipped ashore one dark night with a bag of guineas on his shoulder and not a soul raised a hand to stop him, and I confess I was glad, for he had saved my life twice over. We sailed home rich, and the shares were paid, and some bought farms and some went to sea again, but no one ever dared return to that accursed island. To this day the least thing brings back to me the hoarse song of the blind man's parrot crying pieces of eight, pieces of eight, and the tap tap of the wooden leg going by outside the window in my dreams.",
