        'synopsis' => 'A student gives life to a giant creature and flees from it, learning too late what horror he has made.',
        'text' => "You will rejoice to hear that no disaster has accompanied the start of the enterprise which you have regarded with such evil forebodings. So wrote Robert Walton to his sister from St Petersburg, and his letters are the doorway through which we enter the strangest story ever told. Walton was a young English explorer of boundless ambition who had hired a ship and crew in Russia to sail for the North Pole, through seas of ice where no man had passed, to discover the secret of the magnet or a passage round the world. I shall satisfy my ardent curiosity, he promised her, or fail gloriously. Weeks later, far north among fields and islands of floating ice, his crew spied a strange sight, a sledge drawn by dogs, driven by a gigantic man, racing away over the frozen sea toward the north. The next morning fog cleared from the water and they found a second sledge stranded on a floe of broken ice, its dogs dead, its driver nearly dead too, a thin wasted figure whom they took aboard out of pity.\n\nThe stranger recovered slowly in the warm cabin and would say nothing of himself for days. He asked instead about the ship's course, and when Walton told him of his dream of glory and discovery, the pale man cried out in anguish. Do you share my madness? Have you also drunk of the intoxicating draught? Then he began, slowly, to tell his story, and Walton wrote it all down word by word, and this is that story.\n\nMy name is Victor Frankenstein. I was born in Geneva, in Switzerland, and my family was one of the oldest and most honored in the republic. Before I came into the world my mother had found an orphan in Italy, a beautiful little girl with golden hair and blue eyes, and she adopted her as a gift to me, so that Elizabeth became mine before I could know her, my more than sister, the bright spirit of our house. There was also Henry Clerval, my schoolfellow, a boy of deep imagination who loved stories of chivalry and the old poets, while I burned with questions about the secret causes of things. I asked where the powers of nature lay hidden. I wanted to know why the clouds gathered and poured rain, why rivers froze, what moved the living heart. When I was seventeen my parents sent me to the university at Ingolstadt in Germany, and there my fate overtook me.\n\nThe professors of natural science drew me like a moth to flame. Professor Krempe mocked my old alchemical reading, but Professor Waldman, a mild gray-haired man, told me that modern masters had probed deeper than the ancients ever dreamed. They penetrate into the recesses of nature, he said, and show how she works in her hiding places. I gave myself wholly to anatomy and chemistry, sleeping on a pallet beside my instruments, forgetting food and friends and even the sun itself, until in two years I stood beyond every student of my age. And then I went further than any man has dared to go since. I found the principle of life itself.\n\nI will not describe the workshops of my unhallowed toils, Victor said. In a solitary chamber at the top of the house, apart from every other dwelling, I collected bones from charnel houses and prepared materials for a human body eight feet in height, intending to make it large so that every part might be examined plainly. It was on a dreary night of November that I beheld the accomplishment of my toils. With an anxiety that almost amounted to agony I collected the instruments of life around me, that I might infuse a spark of being into the lifeless thing at my feet. It was already one in the morning. The rain pattered dismally against the panes, and my candle was nearly burned out, when by the glimmer of the half extinguished light I saw the dull yellow eye of the creature open. It breathed hard, and a convulsive motion agitated its limbs.\n\nHow can I describe my emotions at this catastrophe, or how delineate the wretch whom with such infinite pains and care I had endeavored to form? His limbs were in proportion, and I had selected his features as beautiful. Beautiful! Great God! His yellow skin scarcely covered the work of muscles and arteries beneath. His hair was of lustrous black and flowing, his teeth of pearly whiteness, but these luxuriances only formed a more horrid contrast with his watery eyes, that seemed almost of the same color as the dun white sockets in which they were set, his shriveled complexion and straight black lips. I had worked hard for nearly two years, for the sole purpose of infusing life into an inanimate body, and now that I had finished, the beauty of the dream vanished, and breathless horror and disgust filled my heart. Unable to endure the aspect of the being I had created, I rushed out of the chamber, and after long pacing up and down my bedroom I threw myself on my bed in my clothes, seeking a few moments of forgetfulness. But I slept indeed, and dreamed. I thought I saw Elizabeth walking in the streets of Ingolstadt, and I embraced her with delight, and as I kissed her lips they turned livid, and the features seemed to change, and I thought I held the corpse of my dead mother in my arms, a shroud wrapped about her form, and I saw the graveworm crawling in the folds of the flannel. I started from my sleep with horror, and there, in the light of the moon as it forced its way through the window shutters, stood the wretch, the miserable monster whom I had created. He held up the curtain of the bed, and his eyes, if eyes they may be called, were fixed on me. His jaws opened, and he muttered some inarticulate sounds, while a grin wrinkled his cheeks. He might have spoken, but I did not hear. One hand was stretched out, seemingly to detain me, but I escaped and rushed downstairs.\n\nI passed the night in the courtyard below my laboratory in the greatest agitation, listening to every breath of wind that swept past as if it were the dull yellow eye and huge form of my enemy. Morning came pale and cold. I dared not return to the chamber, but when I did at last, the creature was gone, and I threw myself on the bed in joy that the thing had fled. The joy lasted one day. My horror returned with double force, I neglected every outward tie, and my mind sank into sickness. It was Henry Clerval who came at last to Ingolstadt and found me wasted by fever, and it was he who nursed me through long months back to health, hiding from me all news that might wound me, so tender and watchful a friend.\n\nI might have believed my creation only a nightmare, had not grief itself soon proved it real. At the end of my first term a letter from my father called me home in sorrow. William, the sweetest boy that ever laughed, my youngest brother, was dead. Strangled. Murdered on the plain between our house and Geneva as he played after dusk. I rode home through the country where the deed was done, and passing near the spot I saw in a flash of lightning among the pines the figure of a man of gigantic stature, and I knew beyond doubt that the beast I had loosed upon the world had taken the child's life. Then came the deeper horror. Justine Moritz, the gentle girl whom my mother had loved like a daughter and who adored our family, had been found with the locket that hung about little William's neck, and on false evidence and false confession, broken down by her judges, she was condemned and hanged as his murderer. I stood by while an innocent creature died for the crime of my own hands, and I alone knew the true murderer, and no soul on earth would have believed me.\n\nI wandered afterward into the valley of Chamonix among mountains that dwarf all human sorrow, and there amid avalanches and towering ice the whole spirit of the wilderness seemed to mock me. On a glacier high above the sea of ice I saw a figure coming toward me across the snow, superhuman in speed. It was the wretch himself. I cursed him and bade him approach, ready to fight him to the death, and he came on calmly, and even I felt something like pity as he spoke. All men hate the wretched. Yet you, my creator, would tear me to pieces and triumph. Remember that I am thy creature. I ought to be thy Adam, but I am rather the fallen angel, whom thou drivest from joy for no misdeed. He told me his story then, sitting beside me on the ice, and I will set it down briefly here.\n\nHe said that when he escaped my house he had fled into forests near Ingolstadt and lived there in a hut of branches, eating berries and acorns, learning pain and hunger and the kindness of sleep. He found a fire left by wandering beggars and learned its warmth and its cruelty too, for he burned his hand in it like a child. Driven onward he came to a low hovel attached to a small cottage, and through a chink in the plank wall he watched a family, an old blind man and his two children, a young man of great sweetness named Felix and a girl named Agatha, poor peasants who labored hard and wept often, yet loved one another with such tenderness that the monster's heart burned within him. He stole their food at first, then saw their misery and stopped, clearing snow from their path by night and doing secret services. And when a stranger lady arrived, a dark beauty called Safie who could not speak their language, he listened with the family to her lessons and learned speech itself, and learned reading too, and found in a portmanteau in the wood a parcel of books, among them a volume of Plutarch's Lives and Milton's Paradise Lost, which moved him as scripture moves the devout. As false gods had made Adam, so had a false creator made him, he said, and where Adam walked unashamed with his maker, he crept away abhorred.\n\nAt last he gathered courage. When the young people were abroad he entered the cottage and fell at the blind man's feet, saying that he was a traveler and friendless and hunted, and the good old man, who could not see his deformity, listened with pity, and promised help, and called him friend. But Felix returned and saw the monster touching his father, and struck him violently with a stick. I could have torn him limb from limb, the creature said, but my heart sank within me, and I refrained and saw him beat my master's father without revenge. He fled, and when hope dragged him back days later the family was gone forever, the cottage empty, and despair turned his love for mankind to hellish rage. That night he burned the empty cottage to the ground. From that hour, he told me, he had sworn eternal hatred to all men, and above all to the race of Frankenstein, and it was he who had strangled William and planted the locket on the sleeping Justine.\n\nAnd now he made his demand. Every man, he said, has a mate, even the meanest creature, and I am alone. Make me a companion like myself, a female of my own kind, hideous as I am, in some solitary place far from men, and we will go together into the vast wilds of South America and never trouble the world again. If you deny me, he swore, I will work at your destruction, nor finish until I desolate your heart, so that you shall curse the hour of your birth. I refused. He argued, he reasoned, he wept, and his misery conquered my hatred, and I consented, on one condition, that he and his mate should vanish forever from every land of men.\n\nI returned to Geneva and promised my family happiness, and with my father's blessing and Henry for company I set out for England, gathering knowledge on the way for my dreadful task. Leaving Henry with a friend in Scotland I went alone to one of the remote Orkney islands, and in a lonely hut I began the second creature, three feet shorter than the first, but formed with the same terrible art. One night, my lamp nearly burned out, I looked up and saw by its light the filthy demon at the window, grinning and gazing at his intended bride, and a rush of horror flooded me. Might not this new race hate men as he did, and multiply until the earth was cursed? I tore the half finished thing to pieces. The creature gave a howl of devilish despair and departed, and left me a warning in few words. I shall be with you on your wedding-night.\n\nI collected the scattered remains of my second labor and rowed out to sea and sank them in deep water, and returning, I was driven by a storm onto a strange coast, arrested on a charge of murder, and brought before a magistrate in Ireland. The dead man was Henry Clerval, strangled in the night, his silver hair marks on his neck. I raved and ranted and fell into fever for two months, and when my mind cleared I was free, for none could believe the gentle student guilty, but my heart was a desert. I went home and married Elizabeth, for I guessed whose life the threat aimed at, though I said nothing, and armed myself beneath my coat. But the monster sought her, not me. On the night of our marriage, while I patrolled the passages with pistol and dagger, the fiend found her alone in her chamber, and her scream, so terribly unlike her own sweet voice, brought me to the door too late. She hung lifeless at the threshold, her head hanging down and her hair floating about her. My father's heart broke within him, and within weeks he died in my arms.\n\nThen nothing remained but revenge. I gathered the traces of the demon, pursued him month after month over the world, always a day behind him, taunted by carved words in bark telling me to follow, till he led me across the frozen northern ocean, where I took to the dogs and sledge as he had done. I was closing on him when the ice broke and carried us apart, and it was Walton's ship that found me dying on the floe.\n\nHere Victor's strength failed. He begged Walton to swear to finish his work, to kill the enemy he could no longer pursue, and Walton recorded the last scenes. Some nights later, in the cabin where the corpse lay wrapped in sailcloth, Walton heard a sound and found the gigantic creature crouched over the body, weeping, monstrous and miserable even in his grief. He spoke over his victim of his own torments, of the crimes he had hated even while committing them, and declared that his guilt needed no judge. Soon he would die, and the memory of his deeds would haunt him beyond the grave. Farewell, he said to the sleeping Walton, I shall die, and what I now feel be no longer felt. Then springing from the cabin he dropped in a single sledge over the ice, borne away by the waves toward the darkest region of the eternal night, and was seen no more.",
