        'slug' => 'classic-prince-pauper',
        'gutenberg_id' => 1837,
        'title' => 'The Prince and the Pauper',
        'author' => 'Mark Twain',
        'license' => 'Public domain',
        'genres' => ['Historical Fiction'],
        'quiz_difficulty' => 'medium',
        'cover' => 'covers/classic-prince-pauper.jpg',
        'synopsis' => 'A beggar and a prince trade places by chance, and each must survive the other\'s world until truth wins.',
        'text' => "In the ancient city of London, on an autumn day in the year fifteen hundred and forty-seven, two baby boys were born who were as like each other as two pins. One was born to poverty and misery in a wretched court off Pudding Lane called Offal Court, in the family of a poor thief named John Canty. The other was born to riches and glory in Westminster Palace, son of the mighty Henry the Eighth of England. All England wanted the little prince. God save the Prince of Wales rang from every steeple, while people only mocked the other child or paid no heed to him at all.\n\nThe poor baby grew up in Offal Court among the drunkenness and fighting of his father's house. His mother, good Margery Canty, loved him dearly, but his father beat him savagely whenever the mood took him, which was often, and his grandmother beat him worse than his father did. By day he ran about the foul streets ragged and hungry, begging scraps and stealing what he could when his father drove him out to thieve. Yet somehow a light had fallen into this dark life. An old priest named Father Andrew, driven from his church by the new laws of the king, lived humbly in the court, and out of pity he taught Tom Canty his letters and a little Latin, and told him stories of kings and princes until Tom's head was full of them. At night Tom dreamed palaces. He played at being a prince with the neighborhood children, holding mock courts with a broomstick scepter, and he read and reread the three tattered books Father Andrew gave him about royal ceremonies, until he knew how princes sat and walked and spoke better than any boy alive.\n\nThe dream grew until one day Tom found himself starving and footsore, wandering farther than ever before, and suddenly there rose before him the glorious palace of Westminster itself. Gold-tipped gates, towering walls, sentries in shining steel, and beyond, green lawns and marble fountains where peacocks walked like lords. Tom pressed his face against the bars and cried aloud with longing. Perhaps if I could see a real prince only once, he murmured, just once, even at a distance, I should be content for life. The guards pushed him off roughly and laughed at his rags, but just then, behind the gate appeared the Prince of Wales himself, Edward Tudor, splendid in silk and velvet and jewels, and something in the little beggar's face caught him. What would you do with the palace if you had it, asked the prince. The prince opened the gate himself and led Tom in.\n\nInside they stood before a mirror, and there the wonder burst upon them both. Each saw the other's face wearing his own clothes, so alike were they. It seemed a fairy prank played for their amusement, and laughing, they stripped and changed garments. Then the prince remembered a matter of justice he must see to at once, a complaint of the servants, and hurried out still dressed as a pauper, leaving Tom in the royal silks.\n\nWhat followed neither could have dreamed. The palace guard seized the ragged boy at the door, cuffed him soundly for daring to wear the prince's clothes, and pitched him into the street, while within the walls every servant and noble bowed low to Tom and obeyed him, and nothing he could say would persuade anyone he was not the prince himself. When he wept and swore he was only Tom Canty of Offal Court, they exchanged glances of pity. His mind wanders, they whispered, the poor young lord has gone mad with study, and they humored him gently and fed him dainties he had never tasted in his life, and he went to sleep that night between silken sheets more bewildered than any boy in England.\n\nAnd outside those walls the true prince fared no better. Edward wandered the streets in his borrowed rags, hungry for the first time ever, and learned quickly how cruel crowds can be to a beggar boy who talks like a prince. Ruffians jeered him. When he declared his rank they hooted and called him mad, and none mocked louder than John Canty himself, who happened upon him, recognized his own son's face, and dragged him home by the collar to Offal Court to serve as a begging tool, thrashing him when he brought back too little. That same night a great bell tolled through London, and men ran crying in the streets that Henry the Eighth was dead and Edward, Prince of Wales, was now King of England. In the palace, nobles woke Tom Canty with the news and knelt to him trembling. Poor child, they said among themselves, he does not even know he is king, his grief and shock have turned his brain. And Tom lay awake till morning whispering to himself, they will find the true prince tomorrow and put me out, and I shall be glad.\n\nBut tomorrow came, and the next day after, and no one came to put him out. Instead the whole machinery of a mighty kingdom gathered around him to obey his smallest wish. They dressed him in cloth of gold. Learned men waited on his lessons. Great lords carried messages from foreign kings addressed to him. At first Tom was in agony, certain that discovery meant the Tower and the axe, and he begged to be allowed to go home, which confirmed everyone in the belief that he was mad. Little by little, though, the terror wore away and wonder took its place. He was carried to council meetings and heard how England was governed. He signed pardons with his own hand and felt like a god dispensing mercy. He began to learn the ceremonies he had once read of in tattered books, and found he could perform them, for he had rehearsed being a prince his whole childhood in Offal Court games. Days became weeks. The search for the missing prince dwindled quietly, for who would dare suggest that the king was not the king?\n\nMeanwhile the real king of England, a boy of nine in a pauper's rags, was learning what it meant to be poor in his own kingdom. He was beaten for failing to steal. He slept in kennels and ate garbage. He was driven by John Canty with a band of thieves through the countryside, and wherever he went he saw with his own eyes the cruelty of the laws he had never questioned. He saw a woman whipped through the streets for selling goods without license. He heard of men hanged for stealing things worth less than a loaf of bread. Once he watched two humble women burned at the stake for their faith, and the sight seared his soul forever. If ever I am king again, he swore to himself, these things shall be made right, for I have seen how my people suffer and no king who knows it may sleep easy.\n\nIt was during these wanderings that fate sent him Miles Hendon, a soldier newly home from the wars, kindhearted, proud, and honest. He was the younger son of a Kentish gentleman, cast off by an elder brother who had stolen his sweetheart and his inheritance together, and he was riding home after years at the wars to see whether anything was left for him. Hendon found the boy set upon by a mob and rescued him without asking questions, and when Edward commanded him grandly to kneel and serve his king, Hendon laughed at first, then saw that the lad was earnest and perhaps crazed, and resolved to humor him with the respect he craved. So Miles Hendon served the small ragged king as though he were serving a crowned monarch, guarding him from harm, feeding him, standing between him and John Canty's fists, and asking nothing in return but the boy's company. And slowly, in the heart of the haughty little prince, gratitude took root toward the first man who had believed in him enough to be kind.\n\nThe weeks of wandering hardened the little king into something rare, a ruler who knew his people from below. With Miles Hendon guarding him he passed through the country of the gypsies and the farms of Kent, always hunted half the time by John Canty, who still claimed him as property. There was a rascally young thief named Hugo in Canty's gang who tormented the prince with tricks, once pretending wounds to beg alms through fraud, and Edward stormed at such dishonesty until the whole gang laughed at his royal rage. Once Hugo set a bully upon him in a fairground to be rid of him, and Edward, though small, stood up to the man with such fury that even the thieves grudgingly admired him. Through all of it Miles kept his strange promise, calling the boy Your Majesty with a straight face, sleeping beside him in barns, fighting off ruffians when need came, and loving him more each day for the fire that never went out in him.\n\nAnd in Westminster the other boy was ruling England. Tom Canty had discovered that being a king meant hearing petitions all day, and he threw himself into them with a hunger for justice learned in Offal Court. When two poor women were brought before him charged with poisoning, he asked first whether they had been hungry, and the court stood amazed. He heard that the Duke of Norfolk lay condemned in the Tower to die within days, and though he hardly understood the charge against so great a man, some instinct told him the sentence smelled of malice, and he signed the pardon with a shaking hand while the lawyers gasped. He abolished cruel punishments that touched no one else's conscience. He sent food to prisoners starving in the jails. The old statesmen around him began, wonder upon wonders, to admire the boy king's mercy, and the common people, who feel such things quickly, blessed his name in every street of London. It was strange schooling for a beggar's son, but he had been rehearsing kingship in the gutters all his life without knowing it, and now the games became real. He learned to sit the throne without fidgeting, to receive ambassadors without staring, to sign his name in the great round hand that clerks marveled at. Only Tom himself grew quieter as coronation day drew near. The crown was coming, and with it the end of all hope that the true prince would return to claim his own. At night he dreamed of Offal Court and woke weeping for his mother, and he could not even say why, since kings are not supposed to remember mothers in hovels.\n\nCoronation morning rose magnificent. London was hung with banners, the streets swept and graveled, fountains running wine, and along the road to Westminster Abbey rolled the grandest procession England had seen in a lifetime. Tom Canty rode in cloth of gold through roaring crowds, and his misery was hidden under glory. He looked at the thousands of faces streaming past and thought how many of them belonged to people who were hungry that day, and wished with all his heart he could stop the procession and feed them, which is not a thought kings usually think. In the abbey, amid bishops and nobles and the ancient relics of conquest, they brought the crown of the English monarchs near to his head, and Tom closed his eyes.\n\nThen a boy's voice rang through the abbey forbidding the crowning. Guards seized a ragged lad who had slipped from among the spectators, and there stood Edward, tattered, travel-stained, burning with purpose, crying out that he was the true king and demanding the crown. Laughter and amazement shook the hall. But Tom sprang up from the throne, and did the noblest deed of either boy's life. He rushed down from his seat and stood beside the ragged claimant, crying that it was true, this was the rightful king, let him be crowned, for Tom renounced everything and rejoiced to be free of it. Such loyalty stunned the assembly. Here were two faces exactly alike, one claiming kingship, one surrendering it, and neither behaving as impostors behave.\n\nLord Hertford, the prudent uncle, saw that some test must settle it. He turned to Edward with questions only the true prince could answer, and Edward answered them all, but answers prove nothing between twins, said Hertford gently, for your knowledge is shared. Then Edward remembered, and his eyes flashed. Ask the Lord Chancellor where the Great Seal of England lies hidden, he cried, for if I am not the king, no one living knows where it is but me. The chancellor confessed that the Great Seal had been missing since the late king's death and all England had searched for it in vain. I hid it myself before ever I left the palace grounds, said Edward, and he described the place, telling how after the seal was used upon the last writ he had carried it away and thrust it deep inside an old suit of armor's helmet that stood in his private chamber, and jammed the visor shut upon it. Messengers flew to the palace and returned bearing the seal, drawn forth exactly as the boy had said, the great disc of gold with its stamp of state, found jammed in the dusty helmet where no searcher had thought to look, because only a boy who played with armor would hide a kingdom's greatest treasure there. No counterfeit could have known it. The assembly fell upon its knees, and the Archbishop of Canterbury set the crown of England upon the head of Edward the Sixth, true heir of Henry the Eighth, while the trumpets pealed and the crowd outside thundered its joy at news that spread like wildfire.\n\nEdward did not forget what he owed. He made his faithful servant and friend Miles Hendon an earl, with estates fitting the honor, and Miles's elder brother, who had wronged him, found it wise to make his peace, for a brother who is an earl and a favorite of the king is not a man to quarrel with lightly. To Tom Canty the new king showed the same gratitude. He named him King's Ward, a person held in special guardianship by the crown itself, dressed him nobly forever after, and took delight in his honest wit, which had been schooled by a stranger education than any prince ever had. And Tom's good mother, who had mourned her boy, was found and honored too, though she always swore she had known her own child's face in the abbey the moment she saw it, mother's heart being wiser than lords' reasoning.\n\nKing Edward reigned a brief few years, but long enough to change much cruelty into mercy, for he had been a beggar and had seen how the laws ground the poor. The whipping woman, the burned heretics, the men hanged for trifles, all these memories walked with him, and the statutes of his reign show his hand in softening them. Men said afterward that no sovereign of England ever understood his subjects better, and it was true, for he had worn their rags. As for Tom Canty, he lived honored all his days, and the people of London never forgot the boy who had sat upon the throne and given it up gladly, which is more than can be said of many who were born to it.",
