    [
        'slug' => 'classic-call-wild',
        'gutenberg_id' => 215,
        'title' => 'The Call of the Wild',
        'author' => 'Jack London',
        'license' => 'Public domain',
        'genres' => ['Adventure'],
        'quiz_difficulty' => 'medium',
        'cover' => 'covers/classic-call-wild.jpg',
        'synopsis' => 'Stolen from a sunlit ranch, a great dog answers the ancient call of the wild and becomes a legend of the frozen North.',
        'text' => "Buck did not read the newspapers, or he would have known that trouble was coming for every big dog in the frozen North. In the summer of eighteen hundred and ninety-seven men had found gold in the Klondike country of the Canadian Arctic, and the whole world was rushing north with picks and shovels and sleds, and sleds need dogs, strong dogs, heavy dogs with thick coats to stand the terrible cold.\n\nBuck lived at a big house in the sunny Santa Clara Valley of California, Judge Miller's place, and he was king over all it contained. He was born there, a great dog of mixed breed, part St Bernard and part Scotch shepherd, weighing one hundred and forty pounds, and his life was a proud and easy thing. He swam in the swimming tank and hunted with the judge's sons. He guarded the daughters on their long twilight walks among the orchards. He lay at the judge's feet before the library fire through winter evenings, carrying the little grandchildren on his back and rolling with them in the grass. He was lord of the whole domain, dogs and humans alike, and he trusted every man who walked upright, because he had never known anything else.\n\nThat trust was what killed his old life. One night when the judge was away at a fruit growers' meeting, Manuel, the gardener's helper who owed gambling debts he could not pay, led Buck away down the lane to a little flag station where a man waited with a rope and a train ticket. Buck let the rope be slipped around his neck without suspicion, for he had been taught courtesy, but when it tightened and he felt himself flung into the baggage car, his rage was terrible. The man choked him half unconscious and laughed while Buck tore at his hands, and by morning Buck was delivered to a shed in San Francisco where a man in a red sweater looked him over and grinned.\n\nNow this man in the red sweater had a lesson to teach, and Buck learned it once and forever. When Buck sprang at him in fury, the man stepped aside neatly and brought a club down across his head, and Buck fell stunned, then rose and charged again, and again the club crashed down, ten times, twelve times, until the mighty dog lay gasping on the ground and understood dimly that he had met something stronger than any strength of his. The man knelt and patted him and gave him water and meat, not cruelly, only firmly, and Buck took the meat and knew that he had taken his first lesson toward death. The law of club reigned from that hour. A man with a club was a master to be obeyed, though he need not be a master to be loved.\n\nFrom Seattle, days later, Buck passed into the hands of two government dispatch carriers, French Canadians named Francois and Perrault, who bought him along with other strong dogs and carried him aboard a ship bound north. And there on the deck of that vessel he met Curly, a good-natured Newfoundland bitch, and together they watched a husky dog leap upon her without warning or reason. Curly snapped back and stood off as dogs do in friendly play, but that is not the way of the husky, and in a moment thirty or forty of them surrounded the pair, silent and eager, eyes gleaming, lips curling. Curly went down under the rush, and there was no fair fight about it at all. They closed in upon her, tearing and ripping, and when her human protectors drove them off she lay sprawled in the red snow, and Buck learned that here fairness did not exist, that once down was the end, and that he must never go down if he could help it. So that was the way of it, he thought. No fair play. Once down, that was the end of you. Well, he would see to it that he never went down.\n\nThe ship reached Dyea Beach at the head of the Chilkoot trail, and Buck got his first taste of snow, which astonished him, and his first harness, which humiliated him, for he was harnessed between Dave and Sol-leks, two grave experienced sled dogs who cared nothing for anything but their work. Day after day the team hauled mail and outfit up frozen rivers and over mountain passes, and Buck learned the traces as he had learned everything, quickly and thoroughly. He learned to pull at the word mush, to swing wide at curves so the sled would not capsize, to dig his feet into soft snow, to steal food when no eye watched, to sleep curled in a ball beneath the snow itself against the killing cold. His feet hardened till they were tough as horn. His muscles grew like iron cables. The softness of the Southland melted out of him entirely, and something older than civilization rose up inside him, something wild and keen and patient.\n\nAnd with the growth of that old thing came rivalry. Spitz was lead dog of the team, a big white husky from Spitzbergen, grinning always, cunning and pitiless, and from the first day Buck hated him with a hatred that grew in silence. Spitz had laughed, in his wolfish way, when Curly died. Spitz stole Buck's food when he could. Each dog tested the other's strength in small ways, stealing each other's sleeping places, cutting each other out of kills, until the whole team lived in an atmosphere of coming war.\n\nThere were lessons too terrible for forgetting. One day Dolly, who had never given trouble in her life, went suddenly mad on the trail, howling like a thing possessed and flying at every throat within reach. She fixed her fangs on Buck's shoulder before Francois could reach her, and then she turned on Buck himself, chasing him at full speed down the island while the whole team watched from harness. Buck ran as he had never run, through camp after camp, the mad dog's breath hot behind him, until Francois's axe fell at last and ended it. Buck staggered back half dead with exhaustion, and Spitz laughed aloud at his staggering, and Buck marked it against him forever.\n\nThe crisis came in the dead of winter during a rabbit hunt. The team had chased a snowshoe rabbit across miles of white silence, and just as the quarry turned at bay, Spitz cut in from the side and killed it, cheating Buck of the kill that was his due. That was more than the old thing within Buck could bear. With a roar he hurled himself upon the leader, and the team gathered round in a ring to watch, as huskies always gather, silent and waiting. It was a battle such as the Northland rarely sees, tooth against tooth, craft against craft, and twice Buck was nearly beaten, nearly rolled onto his side where the end waits. But in the last moment he feinted low as if for the shoulder, and then drove upward with all his weight behind it, and broke the foreleg of the white dog, and then again, and Spitz went down, and the ring of watching huskies closed in, and Buck stood looking at the fallen king, and the law of club and fang was written plain before him. He was the leader now.\n\nUnder Buck the team changed overnight. He was a born leader, and he made the dogs pull as they had never pulled before, tightening the traces till the sled seemed to fly. Francois and Perrault marveled at their own records. The team moved as one animal now, and even Pike the shirker and Billee the whiner pulled their hearts out under Buck's discipline. They crossed the Thirty Mile River on rotten ice that bent and cracked beneath them, ran through blizzards where men could not see their own hands, and delivered the dispatches in record time, and when at last they reached Skaguay there was pride in every dog of them, for they had done a great thing together.\n\nBut the government had no more need of them, and Buck's heart sank when he saw the new buyers, Scotch halfbreeds who carried mail for the gold fields. The work was heavier than ever, long day after long day hauling loads of letters over the same weary trail, until the spirit went out of the dogs one by one. Dave, the grave wheel dog, grew sick inside and died in harness, falling in his traces and dragging until they shot him mercifully by the trail. Sol-leks mourned him in his silent way. And Buck himself began to feel the old fire dying, his feet sore, his strength ebbing, until the whole team was worn to shadows. Thirty days of continuous hauling, then thirty more, over ice that cut like glass and through drifts that swallowed the sled to its runners, with loads heavier than any dogs should pull, had taken everything from them but the stubborn will to keep moving, and even that was failing now.\n\nThen came the worst masters of all, an American family out of the South, Hal and Charles and Charles's wife Mercedes, who knew nothing of the North or of dogs and would learn nothing. They overloaded the sled until it could not move, then added more. They fed the dogs too little and themselves too much. Mercedes rode on the sled when the dogs were dropping in their tracks, and wept because the dogs would not go faster. When the team fell to fourteen, to nine, to five starving skeletons, Hal bought more dogs with the money he should have spent feeding the ones he had. Buck endured it all with a hatred growing cold and deep, for he had seen death before, but never such stupid cruelty.\n\nThey came at last to the camp of John Thornton, an experienced prospector, lying beside White River with his partners Hans and Pete. Thornton watched the exhausted team crawl past and warned the trio gently that the ice ahead was thin and treacherous, that spring was coming and the river was unsafe. Hal laughed at him. When Buck collapsed and could not rise, Hal took up the club, and Thornton sprang between them with a curse, and struck the club from the man's hand so hard that he staggered. I will kill you if you strike that dog again, said Thornton quietly, and Hal, seeing the man's eyes, thought better of it. Buck lay where he was while the others harnessed what remained of the team, and watched the sled lurch away down the river toward the rotten ice.\n\nHe did not have to watch long. A quarter mile out the whole bottom dropped from under them. Sled, dogs, masters, all went down together into the freezing water, and only a jagged shelf of ice showed where they had been. Buck drew a great breath. John Thornton saved his life that day, and something passed between man and dog deeper than anything Buck had known in Judge Miller's sunny domain. For the first time he loved.\n\nThornton's partners were rough men but honest ones, and the three shared everything, and Buck shared their life through the healing summer. He lay at Thornton's feet by the fire. He swam the wild rivers carrying the man's pack when the raft broke, and once he plunged into boiling rapids where no swimmer could live, and came out with Thornton's arm in his teeth, both of them battered nearly to death, and men talked of that feat for years. Once he fought Black Burton in a saloon for striking Thornton, and broke the bully's throat in a single rush. Once he held a rope in his teeth and let himself be lowered over a cliff edge to haul up a stranded partner. His fame spread through the camps, and men bet on his strength as they bet on nothing else in the North.\n\nThe greatest bet came in Dawson, when Thornton boasted that Buck could start a sled loaded with a thousand pounds of flour. A thousand pounds frozen fast in packed snow, with runners set like welded iron! Men laughed and wagered freely, sixteen hundred dollars against the claim. Thornton knelt by the sled and put his arms around Buck's neck and leaned his head close, not commanding, just loving, and told him softly, As you love me, Buck. As you love me. And Buck, trembling with effort, dug his claws into the snow till they bled, and threw his weight forward inch by inch, and the sled groaned, and the runners creaked loose, and then the load came free and swung ahead, and Buck walked it across the marked distance while the crowd roared itself hoarse. Sixteen hundred dollars in gold dust went into Thornton's pocket, and with it he paid off old debts and staked a search for a lost mine far to the east.\n\nThat mine they found, and that winter they lived rich beyond dreams, but Buck felt another call growing stronger than any harness call. At night he heard voices in the forest, a wild song older than the oldest of his kind, and one evening he met a timber wolf in the moonlight and made friends with him, running beside him for hours through the woods, feeling the old blood leap in answer. He stayed away days at a time now, hunting alone, killing his own meat, growing leaner and fiercer and happier. He learned to stalk the great moose themselves, following a huge bull for days through the timber, wearing him down with patience, harrying him whenever he turned to fight, until at last the giant stood exhausted in a stream bed and Buck sprang upon him as his wild ancestors had sprung since the world was young. Only the love of John Thornton held him to the world of men.\n\nHe was away hunting when the Yeehat Indians raided the camp. He came back to find the lodges torn, the caches scattered, Hans and Pete dead among their weapons, and Thornton gone. Following the trail he found the Yeehats dancing around the wreckage, and what happened then became legend among those tribes for generations. Buck came upon them like the wrath of the wilderness itself, and no man could stand before him. He harried them through the trees till they fled screaming, and he drove them from that valley forever, and then he stood over the place where John Thornton lay and howled the grief of his whole race into the night.\n\nAfter that the last tie was broken. The Yeehats tell of a Ghost Dog that runs at the head of the wolf pack through the moonlit forests, killing the fiercest game, singing the ancient song, and marking its hunting grounds so that men keep clear of them. Each fall the moose run before him, and each winter he sleeps in the snow with his nose to the wind, remembering nothing of Judge Miller's orchards or the red sweater or the law of club, for the memory of those things faded as the call of the wild grew strong, and Buck answered it at last. Yet each summer, it is said, he comes back once to the empty valley where John Thornton died, and stands there a while in silence, and then turns again to his own people in the deep woods, where he is king now as he was king once over another kingdom long ago.",
