        'synopsis' => 'A proud gentleman and a lively young lady must both lay down their pride before love can win them.',
        'text' => "It is a truth universally acknowledged that a single man in possession of a good fortune must be in want of a wife. So the whole neighborhood agreed when word spread through the little town of Meryton that Netherfield Park had been let at last to a young man of large fortune from the north of England. Mr Bingley had four or five thousand a year, said Mrs Bennet to her husband with great excitement, and what a fine thing for our girls. She had five daughters to marry off, Jane beautiful and gentle, Elizabeth lively and quick, Mary bookish and vain of her accomplishments, Kitty giddy, and Lydia wild beyond all governing, and she thought of nothing else from morning till night.\n\nMr Bennet was a strange mixture of quick wit and lazy indifference, teasing his wife endlessly while pretending to refuse her schemes. His wife, a woman of mean understanding and little information, spent her life visiting and news-mongering, and the business of her five daughters occupied her whole time. Elizabeth was her father's favorite, for she had something of his own sharp humor without his idleness. She saw through people quickly, laughed at their follies kindly or otherwise, and prided herself on being a good student of character, a pride that would one day need humbling. But he did call on Mr Bingley as propriety demanded, and soon came the invitation every mother in Meryton awaited, a grand ball where the new neighbor would meet the county. At the ball Bingley danced every dance with Jane and looked at no one else, and everyone saw how things stood between them. His friend Mr Darcy, tall, handsome, and rich enough to be talked of everywhere, was less admired. He danced only once, refused introductions, and walked about the room in proud silence, and when Bingley urged him to ask pretty Elizabeth, who sat without a partner, Darcy glanced at her and answered carelessly within her hearing. She is tolerable, but not handsome enough to tempt me, and I am in no humor tonight to give consequence to young ladies slighted by other men. Elizabeth told the story afterward among her friends as a fine joke, laughing heartily, though something of it stayed with her.\n\nA week later Jane caught a violent cold riding on horseback to dine at Netherfield, and was kept there too ill to go home. Elizabeth walked three miles of muddy lanes to nurse her, arriving with petticoats six inches deep in mud and glowing with exercise, which shocked the fashionable Miss Bingley but secretly pleased Darcy more than he admitted. She stayed some days in that house of strangers, sparring with Darcy over books and character and pride itself, and he found himself watching her fine eyes and listening for her laugh until he grew angry with his own attention. A country girl with vulgar relations, he reminded himself, connections in trade, an absurd mother. It would not do.\n\nThe winter brought two disturbances. The first was Mr Collins, a clergyman cousin who stood to inherit Longbourn itself when Mr Bennet died, since the estate was entailed away from daughters. Collins was a pompous fool who spoke in long prepared speeches and worshipped Lady Catherine de Bourgh, his noble patroness, quoting her opinions on everything from jam to salvation. He decided it was his duty to marry one of the Bennet girls as an apology for inheriting their home, and fixed upon Jane, then upon Elizabeth when told Jane might soon be engaged. He proposed to Elizabeth in a sermon-like address full of reasons why she ought to accept, speaking of her modesty and of Lady Catherine's advice to marry early, and she refused him so plainly that he could not believe his ears, and proposed again, and was refused again. She tried to let him down gently, assuring him she wished him every happiness with some other lady, but his vanity converted every refusal into encouragement, and it took a final unanswerable no to settle the matter at last. Within a fortnight he was engaged to Charlotte Lucas, Elizabeth's sensible plain friend of seven and twenty, who married him purely for security, and told Elizabeth frankly that happiness in marriage is entirely a matter of chance.\n\nThe second disturbance was a soldier. The militia came to winter in Meryton, and among the officers was Mr Wickham, handsome, agreeable, and charming, who paid Elizabeth particular attention and confided in her a tale of wrongs. His father, he claimed, had been steward to old Mr Darcy, who loved him like a son and promised him a valuable living in the church, but the proud son Darcy, jealous of his father's affection, had cheated him of it after the old man died and left him to poverty. Elizabeth believed every word, for it fitted Darcy's pride exactly, and her dislike hardened into contempt.\n\nThen Bingley and his whole party left Netherfield abruptly for London, with no promise of return, and Jane's quiet hope died quietly. Caroline Bingley wrote a few cold lines hinting that her brother might marry Georgiana Darcy someday, and Mrs Bennet raged and lamented while Jane bore it with only reddened eyes. Elizabeth burned with anger on her sister's behalf, certain now that Darcy had separated them out of pride, and when Charlotte asked her whether Jane had shown her feelings enough for even a keen observer to notice, Elizabeth defended her warmly, not knowing how nearly true the criticism fell.\n\nIn spring Elizabeth went to visit Charlotte at her new parsonage near Rosings, Lady Catherine's grand seat, and there the two great threads of her story drew tight. Lady Catherine received them with condescension and questioned Elizabeth rudely about her family and education, and Elizabeth answered with such cool spirit that the great lady was more astonished than offended. And it was at Hunsford that Mr Darcy came again into her life, visiting his aunt Rosings with his cousin Colonel Fitzwilliam. He called often. He watched her. He sought her out in walks and seemed on the edge of speech, and Miss Bingley's letters had meanwhile told him that Jane was in London all winter without calling once, which he took as proof of indifference.\n\nOne evening when Elizabeth sat alone reading, Darcy entered, walked about the room in agitation, and then burst out with a proposal of marriage delivered half as an insult. In vain have I struggled, he said. His sense of her inferiority, of the degradation such a match would bring him, of the obstacles his family would raise, had been dwelt on till it made him miserable, and he could conceal it no longer. He spoke of his love proudly but also of her low connections, and expected acceptance as a favor. Elizabeth refused him with a composure that soon gave way to anger. Had my feelings been favorable, she told him, do you think any consideration would tempt me to accept the man who has been the means of ruining the happiness of my beloved sister? She charged him with separating Bingley from Jane, and with his cruelty to poor Wickham, and Darcy left the room pale with anger and mortification.\n\nThe next morning he found her on a walk and pressed a letter into her hand. It changed everything. In it he owned that he had persuaded Bingley away from Jane, believing her indifferent, but he denied the rest and told the truth about Wickham, who had taken three thousand pounds in settlement of the living, squandered it in dissipation, and then tried to elope with Darcy's young sister Georgiana, barely sixteen, to get his hands on her fortune, and had been stopped only by a timely warning. Elizabeth read it first with disbelief, then with burning shame. She remembered Wickham's easy lies, his attentions shifting to a girl with ten thousand pounds, and she remembered how she had welcomed his story because it flattered her own prejudice. Till this moment I never knew myself, she cried. Pleased with the preference of one and offended by the neglect of the other, she had judged without evidence, vanity on both sides. Her pride was no smaller than his, only better hidden.\n\nThey met no more that spring, and through the summer she toured Derbyshire with her aunt and uncle Gardiner, sensible cheerful people whose trade connections she had once been ashamed of. At her uncle's urging they visited Pemberley, Darcy's magnificent estate, having been assured he was absent. The housekeeper's praise of her master moved her strangely. The best landlord and the best master, said Mrs Reynolds, that ever lived, and not like some young men, proud and ill natured into the bargain. And then Darcy himself appeared, returned a day early, and instead of pride Elizabeth saw confusion, pleasure, and a new humility. He introduced his sister Georgiana to her with warm eagerness, treated her vulgar relations with perfect courtesy, and asked, hesitating, whether she would allow him to introduce his sister to her friends. Something had changed in him, or perhaps had always been changing since a certain refusal.\n\nThen came ruin. A letter from Jane, hurrying after them, brought news that Lydia had run off to Scotland with Wickham, who had left his debts and his regiment together, and there was no word of marriage. The whole family stood disgraced, for everyone knew what such an elopement meant, and every hope of respectable matches for all five sisters died with it. Elizabeth wept over the letter and confessed to Darcy, meeting him by chance, that her family was ruined, and saw his face grow grave and cold, and thought bitterly that now he had reason enough to be glad of her refusal.\n\nBut Wickham had no intention of marrying Lydia, and the Bennets' despair deepened until Mr Gardiner wrote suddenly from London that the couple was found, that Wickham had been brought to marry Lydia on terms settled privately, and that his own debts were paid besides. Mrs Bennet's misery turned overnight to triumph, and Lydia came home brazen and giggling, letting slip in her chatter that Mr Darcy had been at the wedding. Elizabeth wrote to her aunt and learned the truth. It was Darcy who had traced the pair, Darcy who had bought Wickham's compliance at heavy cost, Darcy who had done it all secretly so that even her thanks would be spared him. He had done it for her sake, though she had refused him.\n\nBingley returned to Netherfield in autumn, still loving Jane, and Darcy came with him. This time it was Darcy who encouraged his friend, and within days Bingley proposed and was accepted, and Jane's quiet happiness shone again. Then Lady Catherine herself descended on Longbourn in fury, having heard a rumor that her nephew might marry Elizabeth, and demanded that Elizabeth promise never to accept him. Are the shades of Pemberley to be thus polluted, she cried. Elizabeth refused to promise anything, and sent the great lady off defeated, and afterward laughed that this interference must give Darcy courage if anything could.\n\nIt did. He walked out with her one morning when the others were busy, and spoke simply. You are too generous to trifle with me, he said. If your feelings are what they were last April, tell me so at once. My affections and wishes are unchanged, but one word from you will silence me forever. And Elizabeth, blushing, told him that her feelings were very different, that her heart had long been his, and that his letter and his goodness to Lydia had taught her to know her own mind. He was all delight and gratitude, and owned humbly how properly she had chastened him, teaching him that a gentleman must be worthy of respect as well as born to it. Both were married from the same church in double ceremony, Jane and Bingley laughing beside them, and they lived at Pemberley within thirty miles of Longbourn, happy as only those can be who have earned their happiness by learning to see truly.",
